  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 138 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nEdouard's leftover remnants and moisture envelope has potential to \nunravel and spread a bit more outward as we go from late Thursday \nthrough Friday. Mid-level ridging building in over Oklahoma into \nNorth Texas helps for a southwestward drift of this energy and \nmoisture across our region then into South Texas entering the \nweekend. Weak inverted troughing may then approach on the heels of \nEdouard's remnant energy into early to middle of next week coming \nfrom the northern Gulf waters/coast region. In general, expect for \nthe precipitable water values, mainly along and east/northeast of \nthe I-10/I-37 corridor, to stay slightly a bit elevated for this \ntime of year, in the range of 1.65 to 1.85 inches. While some \nmoisture is in place, the lift for any meaningful pops will remain \nquite nebulous and mostly driven by the heating of the day and \nmesoscale boundaries, such as the sea breeze. This will keep the \narea PoPs mostly in the 10 to 25% range, with the best chances for \nearly to middle of next week mainly concentrated to the coastal \nplains. \n\nThe above average heat remains into the middle of next week. Highs \nremain consistent each day from the mid 90s to around or slightly \nabove the 100 degree mark while the peak afternoon heat indices \nclimb into the 103 to 108 degree range for locations near and east \nof I-35. The overnight lows trend warm in the low to mid 70s for \nmost. Partly cloudy to mostly sunny skies trend with some early \nmorning low clouds at times. A light to moderate southerly to \nsoutheasterly low-level flow maintains through the period.",
